The user just asked for “even more fancy font?” after I’ve already imported the Cinzel Google Font at the top of the style block. I need to actually apply that font to the wordmark text, not just to the SVG logo. 

Looking back at the current CSS in **logo.html**, the wordmark styles are defined under the selector `.ai-wordmark-202606`. Within that block, the font is currently set to a generic system stack:

```css
font-family:system-ui,-apple-system,'Segoe UI',sans-serif;
font-size:calc(var(--logo-size) * .085);
font-weight:250;
letter-spacing:.18em;
text-transform:uppercase;
```

These values were chosen for a clean, modern look, but the user wants something fancier. Since I already added an `@import` for the Cinzel font (a decorative serif typeface) at the top of the stylesheet, the next logical step is to replace the generic font-family with Cinzel, and tweak the typographic metrics (size, weight, letter-spacing) to better showcase the new font’s character. 

I should keep the overall visual hierarchy: the wordmark sits below the logo, so it shouldn’t become too dominant. A slight increase in font-size (from .085 to .088 of `--logo-size`) will make the letters more legible without overwhelming the SVG. Cinzel’s default weight is 400, which is a good balance between too light (250) and too bold; setting `font-weight:400` will let the font’s natural contrast shine. 

Letter-spacing also needs adjustment: Cinzel has a more classical, slightly wider shape, so increasing spacing from .18em to .22em will improve readability and preserve the elegant feel. The other properties (text-transform:uppercase) should stay as they already provide the intended all‑caps style.

Thus, I need to edit the CSS block for `.ai-wordmark-202606` by replacing the old font-family line and the associated size/weight/spacing lines with the new values that reference Cinzel and adjust the metrics. This is a straightforward find‑replace operation that doesn’t require a global replace, only the specific snippet.

Proceed with an Edit tool call targeting the exact old string block and substituting the new string block.

